seg-42e93cd44e94 = Worked example — Kshana as the multi-tool mission-analysis glue layer

# text
# c1: label
seg-286cf0e7bb71 = This walks one trajectory through the standards-based interop and mission-analysis scenario kinds end to end, showing how Kshana sits <em>between</em> the high-fidelity tools a programme already uses rather than competing with them: GMAT (the General Mission Analysis Tool), Orekit and STK (Systems Tool Kit) for trajectory design, and Basilisk or { "42" } for attitude and orbit control system (AOCS) simulation. Every step is a runnable scenario kind, and every result carries a <c1/> stating its scope and evidence tier.

# text
# c1: label
seg-7198d05bece6 = Honesty note: this is a <em>geometry/analytic</em> pipeline at the pre-Phase-A / trade-study tier (before a mission’s first formal design phase). It is not a flight-dynamics certification; see each kind’s <c1/> field.

# text
seg-70c18138497b = Other abbreviations used below: FDS (flight-dynamics system), CCSDS (Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems), OEM (Orbit Ephemeris Message), SGP4 (Simplified General Perturbations { "4" }, the two-line-element propagator), TOML (the scenario file format) and CLI (command-line interface).

# text
seg-7cc0d4d15c4a = The pipeline

# text
# c1: passes
# c2: link-budget
# c3: campaign
seg-09a321d36a10 = The steps are separate runs: each takes its inputs from its own scenario file, so you carry a number from one to the next (a slant range from <c1/> into <c2/>, for example) by editing the next file. The <c3/> kind can hold several kinds in one run under shared conditions; see <a1>CAMPAIGNS.md</a1>.

# text
seg-539ea4e80a77 = Each command below is run from the repository root, and the summary under it is the first line kshana { "0.39.0" } prints for the shipped scenario.

# text
# c1: oem-interop
seg-7939b15235c8 = { "1" }. Ingest the trajectory a designer produced (<c1/>)

# text
seg-4b732dd54291 = GMAT, Orekit and STK all <em>export</em> CCSDS Orbit Ephemeris Messages. Kshana imports them (the other direction of the bridge), so a Kshana analysis can start from the exact orbit the trajectory designer signed off, not a re-derived approximation:

# text
# c1: oem_text
# c2: kshana scenarios/orbit-sgp4-gps.toml --export-oem gps.oem
# c3: oem-interop: ingested 30 segment(s), 10830 states from external OEM (originator KSHANA) (MODELLED interop)
# c4: label
seg-3136cfd5a40e = It reports the segments, objects, frames and epoch span and a velocity-consistency check, showing the ephemeris was ingested faithfully (round-trip error below { "1" }×{ "10" }⁻⁶ km). Given an external file in <c1/> instead, it reports what it parsed: fed the OEM that <c2/> writes, it prints <c3/>. The <c4/> is explicit that this is a structural and physical ingest check, not an orbit-accuracy validation of the source.

# text
# c1: passes
seg-e2c563fcb8cb = { "2" }. When is it visible from a ground station? (<c1/>)

# text
# c1: refraction
# c2: light_time
seg-d66361888ede = Predicts the rise/set passes over a station above an elevation mask: acquisition of signal (AOS), time of closest approach (TCA), loss of signal (LOS), maximum elevation and duration, plus total access time. That is the ground-segment planning query. The orbit is an SGP4 element set carried to the Earth-fixed frame on the IAU 2006/2000A chain, and the elevations are apparent: ITU-R P.834-9 refraction and the downlink light time, each switchable (<c1/>, <c2/>).

# text
# c1: link-budget
seg-24d31bafa334 = { "3" }. Does the contact close? (<c1/>)

# text
seg-47bdb9d4700c = For a pass, put the slant range and the terminal figures into the CCSDS { "401" } / DSN 810-005 (NASA Deep Space Network) link equation:

# text
seg-db12a99edcd2 = Reports free-space path loss (FSPL), carrier-to-noise density (C/N₀), energy per bit over noise density (Eb/N₀), margin and whether the link <b>closes</b> against a required Eb/N₀: the comms feasibility check that turns “it’s visible” into “we can actually downlink the data.” The numbers follow directly from the inputs in the file (here a { "55" } dBW EIRP, effective isotropic radiated power, into a { "53" } dB/K receive G/T, gain-to-noise-temperature ratio); put your own terminal’s figures in before reading the margin. It is a deterministic calculation, not a calibrated terminal datasheet.

# text
# c1: space-weather
seg-85ad46986461 = { "4" }. How fast does the environment decay it? (<c1/>)

# text
seg-8977b12fd934 = Drives thermospheric neutral density from the solar (F10.7, the { "10.7" } cm radio flux, and its 81-day mean F10.7a) and geomagnetic (Kp and ap indices) activity via the Jacchia-71 exospheric temperature. That captures the ~{ "5" }–{ "10" }× solar-cycle density swing a static atmosphere omits, so an orbit-lifetime or drag estimate reflects the space-weather regime rather than a fixed atmosphere. Since { "0.30" } the density is the Jacchia { "1971" } thermospheric density, characterised against NRLMSISE-00 (Naval Research Laboratory mass-spectrometer and incoherent-scatter) but not a data-validated NRLMSISE-00 atmosphere.

# text
seg-4b5bdadf3e7e = { "5" }. Hand the result back (exports)

# text
# c1: orbit
# c2: --export-oem <file>
# c3: --export-sp3 <file>
# c4: --export-omm <file>
# c5: --export <czml|kml|geojson|stk|sigmf|all|list>
# c6: .e
# c7: spectrum
# c8: --export list
# c9: lunar-interop-export
seg-f4a438636ea8 = The same CLI writes standard files for the next tool. On an <c1/> scenario, <c2/> writes a CCSDS OEM { "2.0" } ephemeris with velocity (the file GMAT, Orekit and STK read), <c3/> an SP3 (Standard Product { "3" }) precise ephemeris and <c4/> a CCSDS Orbit Mean-Elements Message catalogue. <c5/> covers the viewers: CZML (Cesium Language), KML (Keyhole Markup Language, for Google Earth), GeoJSON, STK ephemeris files (<c6/>, one per satellite plus the user), and SigMF (Signal Metadata Format) recordings from the <c7/> kind. <c8/> says which apply to a scenario. For the Moon, the <c9/> kind emits the lunar frame, time scale and ephemeris as a CCSDS OEM with a conformance check. See <a1>INTEROP.md</a1>.

# text
seg-5293e6d761a8 = Why this is the glue, not a competitor

# text
# c1: launch-window
# c2: reentry
# c3: eo-coverage
# c4: space-packet
# c5: attitude-budget
seg-cd78b0435abb = Each step is a small, auditable, reproducible scenario with an explicit scope label. The high-fidelity tools own trajectory optimisation, six-degree-of-freedom (6-DoF) AOCS and aerothermal entry, descent and landing (EDL); Kshana owns the <b>open, citable, runnable connective tissue</b>: ingest the standard formats, answer the cross-cutting geometry and feasibility questions, and hand the result on, at a tier any partner can run without a licence. See also the companion mission-analysis kinds <c1/>, <c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/> and <c5/>.

